  "comments": {
    "bugs": {
      "19303": {
        "comments": [
          {
            "tags": [],
            "attachment_id": {},
            "is_private": false,
            "time": "2026-11-12T17:18:27Z",
            "id": 104186,
            "creator": "kevinushey",
            "creation_time": "2026-11-12T17:18:27Z",
            "is_markdown": false,
            "count": 0,
            "text": "The byte code interpreter spends more on each instruction dispatch than it needs to, for a different reason under each compiler:\n\n- gcc keeps a variable in memory for the whole function if it is live across a setjmp call (GCC PR 87466). In bcEval_loop() pc and five other locals are live across the SETJMP of an inline closure call, so every dispatch loads pc from the stack and stores it back. R 4.6.2 is affected too.\n- Recent clang (Apple clang 21, LLVM 22 and 23) emits six to eight register moves before each dispatch, because seven reassigned values are live across it.\n\nTimings: bceval-register-use-bench.R, attached, prints ns per iteration for ten loops. On aarch64 (r90875), loops in a closure are about 22% faster with gcc 13.3 and 16.2 and 13% faster with Apple clang 21; closure calls gain 0 to 4%. x86_64 is not timed; its generated code changes in the same way.\n\nProposed patch: attached as bceval-register-use.diff, against r90877. It reloads the locals after the SETJMP, as was already done for body, and keeps four rarely read locals (body, vcache, smallcache, oldbcpc) in memory; -DR_BCEVAL_SPILL_LOCALS=0 turns the latter off. The cost is a bcEval_loop() stack frame 4 to 13% larger under gcc.\n\nmake check passes with both gcc versions. The patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/469, with the full measurements.",
            "bug_id": 19303
          },
